  "base_commit": "9ae25041c1a993c1ebedcb42630c7107c4ce878c",
  "pr_number": 5525,
  "issue_number": 5507,
  "issue_title": "fix: MCP package's isPublicGitHubProfileUrl mirror is missing the registry's RESERVED_OWNERS exclusion",
  "issue_body": "## Goal\n`packages/mcp/src/public-url-lib.js`'s `isPublicGitHubProfileUrl` should reject GitHub product/marketing pages (`github.com/features`, `/sponsors`, `/about`, `/apps`, etc.) the same way the canonical `packages/registry/src/source-url-lib.js` version does, instead of accepting them as valid GitHub profile URLs.\n\n## Why this matters\n`packages/mcp/src/public-url-lib.js`'s own header comment says it \"Mirrors the registry source-url userinfo guards without importing @heyclaude/registry, so packed tarballs stay self-contained\" \u2014 it's meant to stay behaviorally in sync with `packages/registry/src/source-url-lib.js`. It has drifted: the registry version's `isPublicGitHubProfileUrl` excludes `RESERVED_OWNERS` (github.com/about, /features, /sponsors, /apps, /collections, /contact, /dashboard, /education, and more \u2014 reserved GitHub product pages, not user accounts), but the MCP package's copy has no such exclusion.\n\n`isPublicGitHubProfileUrl` is not dead code \u2014 `packages/mcp/src/submissions-lib.js`'s `isPublicContact` calls it directly to validate a submitter's `contact` field for the MCP-driven submission-preparation tools. Concretely: submitting `https://github.com/features` as a contact URL through the MCP tools passes validation as a \"public GitHub profile,\" while the same value would correctly fail the canonical registry-side validator used elsewhere in the submission pipeline \u2014 an inconsistency between the two entry points for the same submission data.\n\n## Current behavior\n`packages/mcp/src/public-url-lib.js` lines 34-45:\n```js\nexport function isPublicGitHubProfileUrl(value) {\n  const url = parseUrl(value);\n  if (!url) return false;\n  const hostname = url.hostname.replace(/^www\\./i, \"\").toLowerCase();\n  return (\n    url.protocol === \"https:\" &&\n    url.username === \"\" &&\n    url.password === \"\" &&\n    hostname === \"github.com\" &&\n    url.pathname.split(\"/\").filter(Boolean).length === 1\n  );\n}\n```\nNo check against reserved single-segment paths.\n\nCompare `packages/registry/src/source-url-lib.js` lines 142-155, which adds `!RESERVED_OWNERS.has(segments[0].toLowerCase())` (importing `RESERVED_OWNERS` from `./source-repo-lib.js`).\n\n## Desired behavior\nThe MCP package's `isPublicGitHubProfileUrl` rejects the same reserved single-segment paths as the registry version. Since this file is deliberately dependency-free (no `@heyclaude/registry` import, per its own header comment), inline a local copy of the `RESERVED_OWNERS` set (or the minimal subset relevant to profile-vs-product-page disambiguation) rather than importing across packages \u2014 matching how the file already re-implements the userinfo/protocol guards locally instead of importing them.\n\n## Scope\n- `packages/mcp/src/public-url-lib.js` \u2014 `isPublicGitHubProfileUrl` only, plus a local `RESERVED_OWNERS` (or equivalently-named) constant.\n\n## Out of scope\n- Adding a cross-package import from `packages/mcp` to `packages/registry` \u2014 the file's whole purpose is to avoid that dependency for packed-tarball self-containment.\n- Any other function in this file (`hasEmbeddedUrlUserinfo`, `isPublicHttpsUrl`, `publicUrlHostname`) \u2014 those don't have a registry-side reserved-owner concept to mirror.\n- `packages/mcp/src/public-url.js` (the separate re-export/wrapper file) beyond whatever the fixed constant needs.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- PR includes `Closes #<issue>`.\n- `isPublicGitHubProfileUrl(\"https://github.com/features\")`, `.../sponsors`, `.../about` all return `false`.\n- `isPublicGitHubProfileUrl(\"https://github.com/torvalds\")` (a real profile) still returns `true`.\n- A new/updated unit test in `packages/mcp`'s test suite covers at least one reserved-owner rejection case.\n\n## Quality evidence required in the PR\nNo visual impact \u2014 this is a package-internal validation fix with unit-test coverage as the proof, not a UI change.\n\n## Validation\n```\npnpm test:mcp\npnpm validate:mcp-package\n```\n",
